#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119964 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "618f0ac5-e21a-5916-85f1-fbdeee648119" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "057a87a0-f2e0-5ded-9e5b-e55e988beb91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308790} {:answer/id #uuid "0eb353c6-55f3-5856-b487-88af00f0c776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308849} {:answer/id #uuid "ceea24a6-1169-5dfc-96b2-cc63f36822e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310410} {:answer/id #uuid "de4bd802-c89d-5919-bee4-6cbe13e84831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310531} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d5c2bc-9894-547b-9710-238479ebe005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0eb353c6-55f3-5856-b487-88af00f0c776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308849} :answer-field/id #uuid "66524e78-f254-500a-8d22-7a05143b8c39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7180d4ef-44aa-537f-8256-f62f77cfe5b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-336740" :question/problem "![](images/7f/7f997f7647886e1eea24d292ddd66b2d331d6f7d4c7ffe47b6a52b52dd029a44.png)\n\nConsider the RL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-6t}+\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta $ are constants, find the value of $\\alpha +\\beta .$" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $12\\,Ω,$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RL circuit, there are two voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=12\\,Ω.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=12I\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of current $I$ in the circuit is\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)=12I+2\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}+6I=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with constant coefficients. So, to find the general solution, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+6{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as separation of variables, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-6t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a constant of integration.\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $3t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $3t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{p} & =\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t), \\\\ \\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-3\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+3\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))+6(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ (-3\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+(6\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha +6\\beta &=2\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\ 6\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{15},$ $\\beta =\\frac{4}{15}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=-\\frac{2}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-6t}-\\frac{2}{15}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\frac{4}{15}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =-\\frac{2}{15},\\,\\beta =\\frac{4}{15}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\alpha +\\beta  & =-\\frac{2}{15}+\\frac{4}{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338997 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c6044dc-d4a7-5662-a825-3c13e49d198a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets That are Neither Open Nor Closed"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "440337ae-e75b-5e2e-af72-f244faaa1466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467884} {:answer/id #uuid "84120805-e8ea-59f8-8390-7f0edacbe1f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467885}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84120805-e8ea-59f8-8390-7f0edacbe1f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467885} :answer-field/id #uuid "df2c75d9-b4ef-5178-ba10-74ba6baa1d94" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468663} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "afd201de-1dae-5ce1-9c43-571f88d6eff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467886} {:answer/id #uuid "bb427474-e6dd-5ecd-9ad5-db3243527e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afd201de-1dae-5ce1-9c43-571f88d6eff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467886} :answer-field/id #uuid "a36383fc-6ca1-5ff1-8d63-ce4e0dfeba1b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468664} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "703aafea-bae6-565a-8269-49fb586e4b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467888} {:answer/id #uuid "e33ef251-1fda-59d8-80c6-61a0acfd6e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e33ef251-1fda-59d8-80c6-61a0acfd6e0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467889} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b47c5fa-a986-5f5a-a620-2cf86189fd7c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef298213-bf03-538b-9273-2913d7465266" :question/math-academy-id "q-71819" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are neither open nor closed?\n\nI. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0<{y}^{2}\\le 2\\}$\n\nII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0<{x}^{2}<9,\\,1<{y}^{2}<4\\}$\n\nIII. $\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,0\\le x<1,\\,{y}^{2}<4\\}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(1,\\sqrt{2}).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some boundary points, for example, the point $(1,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,0).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(0,2).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}]}
